BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 506.10 Td (Ancient Greek philosophy was divided into three sciences: physics,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 491.10 Td (ethics, and logic. This division is perfectly suitable to the nature of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 476.10 Td (thing; and the only improvement that can be made in it is to add the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 461.10 Td (principle on which it is based, so that we may both satisfy ourselves of) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 446.10 Td (its completeness, and also be able to determine correctly the necessary) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 431.10 Td (subdivisions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 412.10 Td (All rational knowledge is either material or formal: the former) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 397.10 Td (considers some object, the latter is concerned only with the form of) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 382.10 Td (the understanding and of the reason itself, and with the universal laws) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 367.10 Td (of thought in general without distinction of its objects. Formal) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 352.10 Td (philosophy is called logic. Material philosophy, however, which has to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 337.10 Td (do with determinate objects and the laws to which they are subject, is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 322.10 Td (again twofold; for these laws are either laws of nature or of freedom.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 307.10 Td (The science of the former is physics, that of the latter, ethics; they are) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 292.10 Td (also called natural philosophy and moral philosophy respectively.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 273.10 Td (Logic cannot have any empirical part; that is, a part in which the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 258.10 Td (universal and necessary laws of thought should rest on grounds taken) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 243.10 Td (from experience; otherwise it would not be logic, i.e., a canon for the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 228.10 Td (understanding or the reason, valid for all thought, and capable of) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 213.10 Td (demonstration. Natural and moral philosophy, on the contrary, can) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 198.10 Td (each have their empirical part, since the former has to determine the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 183.10 Td (laws of nature as an object of experience; the latter the laws of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 168.10 Td (human will, so far as it is affected by nature: the former, however,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 153.10 Td (being laws according to which everything does happen; the latter, laws) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 138.10 Td (according to which everything ought to happen. Ethics, however, must) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 123.10 Td (also consider the conditions under which what ought to happen) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 108.10 Td (frequently does not.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 89.10 Td (We may call all philosophy empirical, so far as it is based on grounds) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 74.10 Td (of experience: on the other hand, that which delivers its doctrines from) Tj ET
