BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 436.00 Td (e.g., the human will.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 417.00 Td (Now all imperatives command either hypothetically or categorically.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 402.00 Td (The former represent the practical necessity of a possible action as) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 387.00 Td (means to something else that is willed \(or at least which one might) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 372.00 Td (possibly will\). The categorical imperative would be that which) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 357.00 Td (represented an action as necessary of itself without reference to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 342.00 Td (another end, i.e., as objectively necessary.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 323.00 Td (Since every practical law represents a possible action as good and, on) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 308.00 Td (this account, for a subject who is practically determinable by reason,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 293.00 Td (necessary, all imperatives are formulae determining an action which is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 278.00 Td (necessary according to the principle of a will good in some respects. If) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 263.00 Td (now the action is good only as a means to something else, then the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 248.00 Td (imperative is hypothetical; if it is conceived as good in itself and) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 233.00 Td (consequently as being necessarily the principle of a will which of) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 218.00 Td (itself conforms to reason, then it is categorical.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 199.00 Td (Thus the imperative declares what action possible by me would be) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 184.00 Td (good and presents the practical rule in relation to a will which does not) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 169.00 Td (forthwith perform an action simply because it is good, whether) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 154.00 Td (because the subject does not always know that it is good, or because,) Tj ET
