BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 541.00 Td (purpose, but for a purpose which we may presuppose with certainty) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 526.00 Td (and a priori in every man, because it belongs to his being. Now skill in) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 511.00 Td (the choice of means to his own greatest well-being may be called) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 496.00 Td (prudence, * in the narrowest sense. And thus the imperative which) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 481.00 Td (refers to the choice of means to one's own happiness, i.e., the precept) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 466.00 Td (of prudence, is still always hypothetical; the action is not commanded) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 451.00 Td (absolutely, but only as means to another purpose.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 432.00 Td (* The word prudence is taken in two senses: in the one it may bear the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 417.00 Td (name of knowledge of the world, in the other that of private prudence.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 402.00 Td (The former is a man's ability to influence others so as to use them for) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 387.00 Td (his own purposes. The latter is the sagacity to combine all these) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 372.00 Td (purposes for his own lasting benefit. This latter is properly that to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 357.00 Td (which the value even of the former is reduced, and when a man is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 342.00 Td (prudent in the former sense, but not in the latter, we might better say) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 327.00 Td (of him that he is clever and cunning, but, on the whole, imprudent.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 308.00 Td (Finally, there is an imperative which commands a certain conduct) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 293.00 Td (immediately, without having as its condition any other purpose to be) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 278.00 Td (attained by it. This imperative is categorical. It concerns not the matter) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 263.00 Td (of the action, or its intended result, but its form and the principle of) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 248.00 Td (which it is itself a result; and what is essentially good in it consists in) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 233.00 Td (the mental disposition, let the consequence be what it may. This) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 218.00 Td (imperative may be called that of morality.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 199.00 Td (There is a marked distinction also between the volitions on these three) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 184.00 Td (sorts of principles in the dissimilarity of the obligation of the will. In) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 169.00 Td (order to mark this difference more clearly, I think they would be most) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 154.00 Td (suitably named in their order if we said they are either rules of skill, or) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 139.00 Td (counsels of prudence, or commands \(laws\) of morality. For it is law) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 124.00 Td (only that involves the conception of an unconditional and objective) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 109.00 Td (necessity, which is consequently universally valid; and commands are) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 94.00 Td (laws which must be obeyed, that is, must be followed, even in) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 79.00 Td (opposition to inclination. Counsels, indeed, involve necessity, but one) Tj ET
