BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 372.00 Td (since a will so dependent would itself still need another law restricting) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 357.00 Td (the interest of its self-love by the condition that it should be valid as) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 342.00 Td (universal law.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 323.00 Td (Thus the principle that every human will is a will which in all its) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 308.00 Td (maxims gives universal laws, * provided it be otherwise justified,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 293.00 Td (would be very well adapted to be the categorical imperative, in this) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 278.00 Td (respect, namely, that just because of the idea of universal legislation it) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 263.00 Td (is not based on interest, and therefore it alone among all possible) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 248.00 Td (imperatives can be unconditional. Or still better, converting the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 233.00 Td (proposition, if there is a categorical imperative \(i.e., a law for the will) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 218.00 Td (of every rational being\), it can only command that everything be done) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 203.00 Td (from maxims of one's will regarded as a will which could at the same) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 188.00 Td (time will that it should itself give universal laws, for in that case only) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 173.00 Td (the practical principle and the imperative which it obeys are) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 54.00 158.00 Td (unconditional, since they cannot be based on any interest.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 68.00 139.00 Td (* I may be excused from adducing examples to elucidate this) Tj ET
